.SH NAME
frontend/node_modules/cmdk Directory Reference
.SH SYNOPSIS
.br
.PP
.SH "Detailed Description"
.PP 


.PP
 
.SH "⌘K \fR\fP \fR\fP"
.PP
⌘K is a command menu React component that can also be used as an accessible combobox\&. You render items, it filters and sorts them automatically\&. ⌘K supports a fully composable API \*{\*{\fBHow?\fP\*} \*} , so you can wrap items in other components or even as static JSX\&.

.PP
Demo and examples: \fRcmdk\&.paco\&.me\fP
.SS "Install"
.PP
.nf
pnpm install cmdk
.fi
.PP
.SS "Use"
.PP
.nf
import { Command } from 'cmdk'

const CommandMenu = () => {
  return (
    <Command label="Command Menu">
      <Command\&.Input />
      <Command\&.List>
        <Command\&.Empty>No results found\&.</Command\&.Empty>

        <Command\&.Group heading="Letters">
          <Command\&.Item>a</Command\&.Item>
          <Command\&.Item>b</Command\&.Item>
          <Command\&.Separator />
          <Command\&.Item>c</Command\&.Item>
        </Command\&.Group>

        <Command\&.Item>Apple</Command\&.Item>
      </Command\&.List>
    </Command>
  )
}
.fi
.PP

.PP
Or in a dialog:

.PP
.PP
.nf
import { Command } from 'cmdk'

const CommandMenu = () => {
  const [open, setOpen] = React\&.useState(false)

  // Toggle the menu when ⌘K is pressed
  React\&.useEffect(() => {
    const down = (e) => {
      if (e\&.key === 'k' && (e\&.metaKey || e\&.ctrlKey)) {
        e\&.preventDefault()
        setOpen((open) => !open)
      }
    }

    document\&.addEventListener('keydown', down)
    return () => document\&.removeEventListener('keydown', down)
  }, [])

  return (
    <Command\&.Dialog open={open} onOpenChange={setOpen} label="Global Command Menu">
      <Command\&.Input />
      <Command\&.List>
        <Command\&.Empty>No results found\&.</Command\&.Empty>

        <Command\&.Group heading="Letters">
          <Command\&.Item>a</Command\&.Item>
          <Command\&.Item>b</Command\&.Item>
          <Command\&.Separator />
          <Command\&.Item>c</Command\&.Item>
        </Command\&.Group>

        <Command\&.Item>Apple</Command\&.Item>
      </Command\&.List>
    </Command\&.Dialog>
  )
}
.fi
.PP
.SS "Parts and styling"
All parts forward props, including \fRref\fP, to an appropriate element\&. Each part has a specific data-attribute (starting with \fRcmdk-\fP) that can be used for styling\&.
.SS "Command \fR[cmdk-root]\fP"
Render this to show the command menu inline, or use \fBDialog\fP to render in a elevated context\&. Can be controlled with the \fRvalue\fP and \fRonValueChange\fP props\&.

.PP
.RS 4
\fBNote\fP

.PP
Values are always trimmed with the \fRtrim()\fP method\&. 
.RE
.PP

.PP
.PP
.nf
const [value, setValue] = React\&.useState('apple')

return (
  <Command value={value} onValueChange={setValue}>
    <Command\&.Input />
    <Command\&.List>
      <Command\&.Item>Orange</Command\&.Item>
      <Command\&.Item>Apple</Command\&.Item>
    </Command\&.List>
  </Command>
)
.fi
.PP

.PP
You can provide a custom \fRfilter\fP function that is called to rank each item\&. Note that the value will be trimmed\&.

.PP
.PP
.nf
<Command
  filter={(value, search) => {
    if (value\&.includes(search)) return 1
    return 0
  }}
/>
.fi
.PP

.PP
A third argument, \fRkeywords\fP, can also be provided to the filter function\&. Keywords act as aliases for the item value, and can also affect the rank of the item\&. Keywords are trimmed\&.

.PP
.PP
.nf
<Command
  filter={(value, search, keywords) => {
    const extendValue = value + ' ' + keywords\&.join(' ')
    if (extendValue\&.includes(search)) return 1
    return 0
  }}
/>
.fi
.PP

.PP
Or disable filtering and sorting entirely:

.PP
.PP
.nf
<Command shouldFilter={false}>
  <Command\&.List>
    {filteredItems\&.map((item) => {
      return (
        <Command\&.Item key={item} value={item}>
          {item}
        </Command\&.Item>
      )
    })}
  </Command\&.List>
</Command>
.fi
.PP

.PP
You can make the arrow keys wrap around the list (when you reach the end, it goes back to the first item) by setting the \fRloop\fP prop:

.PP
.PP
.nf
<Command loop />
.fi
.PP
.SS "Dialog \fR[cmdk-dialog]\fP \fR[cmdk-overlay]\fP"
Props are forwarded to \fBCommand\fP\&. Composes Radix UI's Dialog component\&. The overlay is always rendered\&. See the \fRRadix Documentation\fP for more information\&. Can be controlled with the \fRopen\fP and \fRonOpenChange\fP props\&.

.PP
.PP
.nf
const [open, setOpen] = React\&.useState(false)

return (
  <Command\&.Dialog open={open} onOpenChange={setOpen}>
    \&.\&.\&.
  </Command\&.Dialog>
)
.fi
.PP

.PP
You can provide a \fRcontainer\fP prop that accepts an HTML element that is forwarded to Radix UI's Dialog Portal component to specify which element the Dialog should portal into (defaults to \fRbody\fP)\&. See the \fRRadix Documentation\fP for more information\&.

.PP
.PP
.nf
const containerElement = React\&.useRef(null)

return (
  <>
    <Command\&.Dialog container={containerElement\&.current} />
    <div ref={containerElement} />
  </>
)
.fi
.PP
.SS "Input \fR[cmdk-input]\fP"
All props are forwarded to the underlying \fRinput\fP element\&. Can be controlled with the \fRvalue\fP and \fRonValueChange\fP props\&.

.PP
.PP
.nf
const [search, setSearch] = React\&.useState('')

return <Command\&.Input value={search} onValueChange={setSearch} />
.fi
.PP
.SS "List \fR[cmdk-list]\fP"
Contains items and groups\&. Animate height using the \fR--cmdk-list-height\fP CSS variable\&.

.PP
.PP
.nf
[cmdk\-list] {
  min\-height: 300px;
  height: var(\-\-cmdk\-list\-height);
  max\-height: 500px;
  transition: height 100ms ease;
}
.fi
.PP

.PP
To scroll item into view earlier near the edges of the viewport, use scroll-padding:

.PP
.PP
.nf
[cmdk\-list] {
  scroll\-padding\-block\-start: 8px;
  scroll\-padding\-block\-end: 8px;
}
.fi
.PP
.SS "Item \fR[cmdk-item]\fP \fR[data-disabled?]\fP \fR[data-selected?]\fP"
Item that becomes active on pointer enter\&. You should provide a unique \fRvalue\fP for each item, but it will be automatically inferred from the \fR\&.textContent\fP\&.

.PP
.PP
.nf
<Command\&.Item
  onSelect={(value) => console\&.log('Selected', value)}
  // Value is implicity "apple" because of the provided text content
>
  Apple
</Command\&.Item>
.fi
.PP

.PP
You can also provide a \fRkeywords\fP prop to help with filtering\&. Keywords are trimmed\&.

.PP
.PP
.nf
<Command\&.Item keywords={['fruit', 'apple']}>Apple</Command\&.Item>
.fi
.PP

.PP
.PP
.nf
<Command\&.Item
  onSelect={(value) => console\&.log('Selected', value)}
  // Value is implicity "apple" because of the provided text content
>
  Apple
</Command\&.Item>
.fi
.PP

.PP
You can force an item to always render, regardless of filtering, by passing the \fRforceMount\fP prop\&.
.SS "Group \fR[cmdk-group]\fP \fR[hidden?]\fP"
Groups items together with the given \fRheading\fP (\fR[cmdk-group-heading]\fP)\&.

.PP
.PP
.nf
<Command\&.Group heading="Fruit">
  <Command\&.Item>Apple</Command\&.Item>
</Command\&.Group>
.fi
.PP

.PP
Groups will not unmount from the DOM, rather the \fRhidden\fP attribute is applied to hide it from view\&. This may be relevant in your styling\&.

.PP
You can force a group to always render, regardless of filtering, by passing the \fRforceMount\fP prop\&.
.SS "Separator \fR[cmdk-separator]\fP"
Visible when the search query is empty or \fRalwaysRender\fP is true, hidden otherwise\&.
.SS "Empty \fR[cmdk-empty]\fP"
Automatically renders when there are no results for the search query\&.
.SS "Loading \fR[cmdk-loading]\fP"
You should conditionally render this with \fRprogress\fP while loading asynchronous items\&.

.PP
.PP
.nf
const [loading, setLoading] = React\&.useState(false)

return <Command\&.List>{loading && <Command\&.Loading>Hang on…</Command\&.Loading>}</Command\&.List>
.fi
.PP
.SS "\fRuseCommandState(state => state\&.selectedField)\fP"
Hook that composes \fR\fRuseSyncExternalStore\fP\fP\&. Pass a function that returns a slice of the command menu state to re-render when that slice changes\&. This hook is provided for advanced use cases and should not be commonly used\&.

.PP
A good use case would be to render a more detailed empty state, like so:

.PP
.PP
.nf
const search = useCommandState((state) => state\&.search)
return <Command\&.Empty>No results found for "{search}"\&.</Command\&.Empty>
.fi
.PP
.SS "Examples"
Code snippets for common use cases\&.
.SS "Nested items"
Often selecting one item should navigate deeper, with a more refined set of items\&. For example selecting "Change theme…" should show new items "Dark theme" and "Light theme"\&. We call these sets of items "pages", and they can be implemented with simple state:

.PP
.PP
.nf
const ref = React\&.useRef(null)
const [open, setOpen] = React\&.useState(false)
const [search, setSearch] = React\&.useState('')
const [pages, setPages] = React\&.useState([])
const page = pages[pages\&.length \- 1]

return (
  <Command
    onKeyDown={(e) => {
      // Escape goes to previous page
      // Backspace goes to previous page when search is empty
      if (e\&.key === 'Escape' || (e\&.key === 'Backspace' && !search)) {
        e\&.preventDefault()
        setPages((pages) => pages\&.slice(0, \-1))
      }
    }}
  >
    <Command\&.Input value={search} onValueChange={setSearch} />
    <Command\&.List>
      {!page && (
        <>
          <Command\&.Item onSelect={() => setPages([\&.\&.\&.pages, 'projects'])}>Search projects…</Command\&.Item>
          <Command\&.Item onSelect={() => setPages([\&.\&.\&.pages, 'teams'])}>Join a team…</Command\&.Item>
        </>
      )}

      {page === 'projects' && (
        <>
          <Command\&.Item>Project A</Command\&.Item>
          <Command\&.Item>Project B</Command\&.Item>
        </>
      )}

      {page === 'teams' && (
        <>
          <Command\&.Item>Team 1</Command\&.Item>
          <Command\&.Item>Team 2</Command\&.Item>
        </>
      )}
    </Command\&.List>
  </Command>
)
.fi
.PP
.SS "Show sub-items when searching"
If your items have nested sub-items that you only want to reveal when searching, render based on the search state:

.PP
.PP
.nf
const SubItem = (props) => {
  const search = useCommandState((state) => state\&.search)
  if (!search) return null
  return <Command\&.Item {\&.\&.\&.props} />
}

return (
  <Command>
    <Command\&.Input />
    <Command\&.List>
      <Command\&.Item>Change theme…</Command\&.Item>
      <SubItem>Change theme to dark</SubItem>
      <SubItem>Change theme to light</SubItem>
    </Command\&.List>
  </Command>
)
.fi
.PP
.SS "Asynchronous results"
Render the items as they become available\&. Filtering and sorting will happen automatically\&.

.PP
.PP
.nf
const [loading, setLoading] = React\&.useState(false)
const [items, setItems] = React\&.useState([])

React\&.useEffect(() => {
  async function getItems() {
    setLoading(true)
    const res = await api\&.get('/dictionary')
    setItems(res)
    setLoading(false)
  }

  getItems()
}, [])

return (
  <Command>
    <Command\&.Input />
    <Command\&.List>
      {loading && <Command\&.Loading>Fetching words…</Command\&.Loading>}
      {items\&.map((item) => {
        return (
          <Command\&.Item key={`word\-${item}`} value={item}>
            {item}
          </Command\&.Item>
        )
      })}
    </Command\&.List>
  </Command>
)
.fi
.PP
.SS "Use inside Popover"
We recommend using the \fRRadix UI popover\fP component\&. ⌘K relies on the Radix UI Dialog component, so this will reduce your bundle size a bit due to shared dependencies\&.

.PP
.PP
.nf
$ pnpm install @radix\-ui/react\-popover
.fi
.PP

.PP
Render \fRCommand\fP inside of the popover content:

.PP
.PP
.nf
import * as Popover from '@radix\-ui/react\-popover'

return (
  <Popover\&.Root>
    <Popover\&.Trigger>Toggle popover</Popover\&.Trigger>

    <Popover\&.Content>
      <Command>
        <Command\&.Input />
        <Command\&.List>
          <Command\&.Item>Apple</Command\&.Item>
        </Command\&.List>
      </Command>
    </Popover\&.Content>
  </Popover\&.Root>
)
.fi
.PP
.SS "Drop in stylesheets"
You can find global stylesheets to drop in as a starting point for styling\&. See \fRwebsite/styles/cmdk\fP for examples\&.
.SS "FAQ"
\fBAccessible?\fP Yes\&. Labeling, aria attributes, and DOM ordering tested with Voice Over and Chrome DevTools\&. \fBDialog\fP composes an accessible Dialog implementation\&.

.PP
\fBVirtualization?\fP No\&. Good performance up to 2,000-3,000 items, though\&. Read below to bring your own\&.

.PP
\fBFilter/sort items manually?\fP Yes\&. Pass \fRshouldFilter={false}\fP to \fBCommand\fP\&. Better memory usage and performance\&. Bring your own virtualization this way\&.

.PP
\fBReact 18 safe?\fP Yes, required\&. Uses React 18 hooks like \fRuseId\fP and \fRuseSyncExternalStore\fP\&.

.PP
\fBUnstyled?\fP Yes, use the listed CSS selectors\&.

.PP
\fBHydration mismatch?\fP No, likely a bug in your code\&. Ensure the \fRopen\fP prop to \fRCommand\&.Dialog\fP is \fRfalse\fP on the server\&.

.PP
\fBReact strict mode safe?\fP Yes\&. Open an issue if you notice an issue\&.

.PP
\fBWeird/wrong behavior?\fP Make sure your \fRCommand\&.Item\fP has a \fRkey\fP and unique \fRvalue\fP\&.

.PP
\fBConcurrent mode safe?\fP Maybe, but concurrent mode is not yet real\&. Uses risky approaches like manual DOM ordering\&.

.PP
\fBReact server component?\fP No, it's a client component\&.

.PP
\fBListen for ⌘K automatically?\fP No, do it yourself to have full control over keybind context\&.

.PP
\fBReact Native?\fP No, and no plans to support it\&. If you build a React Native version, let us know and we'll link your repository here\&.
.SS "History"
Written in 2019 by Paco (\fR@pacocoursey\fP) to see if a composable combobox API was possible\&. Used for the Vercel command menu and autocomplete by Rauno (\fR@raunofreiberg\fP) in 2020\&. Re-written independently in 2022 with a simpler and more performant approach\&. Ideas and help from Shu (\fR@shuding_\fP)\&.

.PP
\fRuse-descendants\fP was extracted from the 2019 version\&.
.SS "Testing"
First, install dependencies and Playwright browsers:

.PP
.PP
.nf
pnpm install
pnpm playwright install
.fi
.PP

.PP
Then ensure you've built the library:

.PP
.PP
.nf
pnpm build
.fi
.PP

.PP
Then run the tests using your local build against real browser engines:

.PP
.PP
.nf
pnpm test
.fi
.PP
 
